// Provider bounded context — see PDD §7.2 (service inventory entry #4)
// and §8.2 (`provider` schema column inventory).
//
// Scope so far:
//   - TS-050 added the `provider` Postgres schema and the core
//     `providers` profile table — the row that backs every chef /
//     culinary-companion / caregiver listing on the platform. Two
//     enums (`provider_status`, `provider_tier`) bound the state
//     machine and the marketplace pricing band (PRD §5.2: Basic /
//     Certified / Elite). Indexes cover the four hot read paths.
//
//   - TS-051 adds the application + background-check tables.
//     `provider_applications` records every formal application a
//     provider submits (PRD §7.1) — once submitted the row sits in
//     `submitted` until ops review (TS-052) closes it. The
//     `provider_background_checks` row pairs with each application;
//     it holds the Checkr candidate + report identifiers, the rolled-
//     up Checkr status, and an at-rest-encrypted copy of the raw
//     Checkr event payload (PDD §16.2). New enums:
//     `application_status` (submitted | in_review | approved |
//     rejected | withdrawn) and `background_check_status` (pending |
//     processing | clear | consider | suspended | engaged | dispute |
//     canceled | failed). The applicant PII (legal name, DOB, SSN,
//     address) is NEVER persisted on our side — Checkr receives it
//     directly via the candidate-create call and we keep only the
//     opaque `checkr_candidate_id`. CLAUDE.md §17.1 / §3.5.
//
// Deliberately deferred — each follow-up arrives as an *additive*
// expand → migrate → contract migration (CLAUDE.md §4.1) so existing
// rows stay readable:
//
//   - TS-052 lands the **certifications + tier-promotion** machinery
//     additively. New tables `certifications` (catalog),
//     `provider_certifications` (per-provider issuance log with
//     issued/expires/revoked timestamps), and `provider_tier_history`
//     (append-only audit of tier transitions). New enum
//     `tier_transition_reason` (`auto_evaluation` | `admin_override`).
//     The pre-existing `providers.tier` column is the live source of
//     truth; the tier-history table records each transition. Tier
//     promotion rules: `basic` (default), `certified` (requires the
//     Certified Culinary Companion cert), `elite` (requires CCC + the
//     Elite Concierge Provider cert). `provider_specialties` /
//     `provider_documents` (food-handler cert, insurance proof) stay
//     deferred until `media-svc` (TS-110) is online for signed-URL
//     issuance + scan-status tracking.
//   - TS-200 lands the self-service **profile editor** machinery.
//     Adds the `provider_profile_tags(provider_id, kind, tag)`
//     polymorphic table (one row per (provider, kind, tag) triple;
//     kinds: `language` / `cuisine` / `dietary_expertise`), the
//     matching `provider_profile_tag_kind` enum, and the
//     `providers.dementia_sensitive` boolean column. The polymorphic
//     shape collapses what PDD §8.2 originally framed as three
//     sibling tables (`provider_languages` /
//     `provider_cuisines` / `provider_dietary_expertise`) into one
//     table per CLAUDE.md §2.3 scope-discipline (one migration / one
//     repository / one outbox composition). The
//     `provider.profile_updated` event fires inside the profile-
//     update transaction; the search-indexer (TS-053) treats it as a
//     "re-fetch + re-project" trigger.
//   - TS-202 — provider service-area self-service editor. Lands the
//     `provider_service_areas` table (one row per declared coverage
//     polygon; geo stored as GeoJSON in a `jsonb` column, with the
//     planar area-weighted centroid + axis-aligned bounding box
//     computed in application code at write time and persisted as
//     `double precision` columns — no PostGIS extension on the Phase-1
//     self-managed Postgres). The search-indexer (TS-053-followup-3)
//     reads the centroid into `ProviderDiscoveryDocument.centroid`;
//     polygon-intersection / geo-distance scoring lands with TS-210.
//     The `provider.service_areas_updated` event fires inside the
//     service-area-update transaction; consumers re-fetch the
//     discovery snapshot to re-project (PDD §8.5 / §14.1).
//   - TS-203 — provider availability self-service editor. Lands the
//     `provider_availability_windows` table (one row per recurring
//     weekly `(weekday, start_time, end_time)` triple, with multiple
//     non-overlapping windows allowed per weekday) and the
//     `provider_availability_exceptions` table (one row per
//     date-keyed all-day blackout). New enum
//     `provider_availability_weekday` with seven lowercased English
//     literals. The discovery-snapshot endpoint projects the next-7-
//     days resolved availability from these rows into the
//     `ProviderDiscoveryDocument.availabilitySummary` field that the
//     search-indexer (TS-053) consumes. The
//     `provider.availability_updated` event fires inside the
//     availability-update transaction; consumers re-fetch the
//     discovery snapshot to re-project.
//   - TS-204 — provider pricing-band editor. Adds the `hourly_rate`
//     `Decimal(12,2)` + `hourly_rate_currency` `Char(3)` columns to
//     `providers` (both nullable; set / cleared together). The write
//     path enforces the rate sits inside the platform band for the
//     provider's `tier`; the `provider.pricing_updated` event fires
//     inside the pricing-update transaction.
//   - TS-090 — Stripe Connect Express payout. Adds payout-method
//     status columns; the actual `stripe_account_id` lives in the
//     payouts service per the cross-service-FK convention.
//   - `provider_metrics` LANDED under TS-305d, and its shape differs
//     from the sketch above in two ways worth stating here. There is
//     **no `rating_avg`** — nothing on this platform captures a
//     rating, and a nullable column would read as "no rating for this
//     provider" when the truth is "we do not collect ratings"
//     (TS-305e). And the table is fed by a second, new table,
//     `provider_booking_facts` (one row per booking), because
//     `response_time_p50` and any rolling window are not computable
//     from counters alone. `provider_metrics` therefore holds only
//     LIFETIME figures, which cannot go stale; windowed ones are
//     derived at read time from the facts. See both models' doc-blocks.
//     This also made service-provider a first-time outbox CONSUMER —
//     hence `outbox_consumer_dedup` alongside the producer-side
//     `outbox_events`.
//
// Cross-service references are by id only — never a foreign key into
// another service schema (CLAUDE.md §2.3, §4.1). Specifically:
//
//   - `providers.user_id` is a soft FK into `identity.users.id`.
//   - `provider_applications.reviewer_user_id` is a soft FK into
//     `identity.users.id` (set by ops staff during review).
//
// Joins across these boundaries happen via the gateway BFF (TS-140)
// or via events (TS-142), never via SQL JOIN.

generator client {
  provider        = "prisma-client-js"
  binaryTargets   = ["native", "linux-musl-openssl-3.0.x"]
  // Generated into the service's own `prisma/generated` rather than
  // `node_modules/.prisma/client`. Under pnpm all 20 services share ONE
  // physical `@prisma/client` in the store, so `require('.prisma/client')`
  // resolves up from the store realpath and finds the 2 KB postinstall stub
  // (0 models) instead — every service then dies at boot with
  // "@prisma/client did not initialize yet" (TS-500).
  //
  // `./generated` is relative to THIS file, so it lands at
  // `<service>/prisma/generated`. The import path `../../prisma/generated`
  // is then identical from `src/prisma/` and from the compiled
  // `dist/prisma/` — one path that works at type-check time and at runtime.
  output          = "./generated"
  // `multiSchema` is required for `@@schema(...)`. The feature has
  // been production-stable since Prisma 5.0 even though it remains
  // under the `previewFeatures` umbrella in 5.x.
  previewFeatures = ["multiSchema"]
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
  // The service owns the `provider` Postgres schema. Every other
  // service gets its own schema in the same database (Phase 1) or
  // its own database entirely (Phase 3 — see PDD §7.2 final
  // paragraph).
  schemas  = ["provider"]
}

/// Provider lifecycle status. Drives whether the profile is bookable,
/// surfaced in search, and whether tier promotions are allowed.
/// Distinct from `tier` because a provider can sit at any tier while
/// not-yet-bookable (e.g. background check pending after a tier
/// upgrade).
///
/// `pending`    = provider row exists but no application submitted
///                (transitional shape; today the row is created at
///                application-submit time so the row never sits in
///                `pending` in practice — captured for forward-
///                compatibility with a future "save draft profile"
///                flow).
/// `in_review`  = application submitted; background check started;
///                ops queue (PRD §10.7) reviewing. The profile is
///                invisible to search and no bookings accepted.
/// `active`     = approved; bookable; surfaced in search subject to
///                tier filters. The normal steady state.
/// `suspended`  = temporary hold (welfare-concern flag, complaint
///                under review, certification lapse). Profile pulled
///                from search; existing recurring bookings honour
///                their own cancellation policy rather than auto-
///                cancel. Resolvable back to `active` via TS-052
///                admin tooling.
/// `archived`   = provider left / was terminated. No further
///                activity; row retained for audit / accounting
///                historical reads.
enum ProviderStatus {
  pending
  in_review
  active
  suspended
  archived

  @@map("provider_status")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider tier — PRD §5.2 / §5.4 pricing & commission bands.
///
///   `basic`     — $29/mo subscription; 30% marketplace commission;
///                 standard listing. Default for newly-onboarded
///                 providers until their first Cooking Academy
///                 certification lands (TS-052).
///   `certified` — $99/mo subscription; 20% marketplace commission;
///                 featured placement; premium listing badge.
///                 Requires Taste & See Cooking Academy certification
///                 (CCC).
///   `elite`     — $199–$499/mo; 10% marketplace commission; luxury-
///                 tier client access; concierge-tier placements.
///                 Requires ECC certification + advanced credentials.
///
/// Tier is a marketplace pricing/commission band. The booking service
/// (TS-064) enforces the "Tier 3 (Concierge) households can only book
/// Elite providers" rule at the service layer (CLAUDE.md §12). Tier
/// transitions write to a history table once TS-052 lands; today the
/// column is set at creation and updated by admin tooling alone.
enum ProviderTier {
  basic
  certified
  elite

  @@map("provider_tier")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider-application lifecycle status (TS-051).
///
/// `submitted`  = applicant has POSTed the application; background
///                check is being initiated. The default state on
///                insert.
/// `in_review`  = background check returned (clear / consider / etc.)
///                and ops is now actively reviewing the application.
///                The transition is owned by TS-052 admin tooling.
/// `approved`   = ops accepted the application; the matching
///                `providers.status` flips to `active`.
/// `rejected`   = ops declined the application; the matching
///                `providers.status` stays `pending` (or moves to
///                `archived` on a terminal decision).
/// `withdrawn`  = applicant withdrew their application before any
///                ops decision was rendered.
///
/// The terminal-state transitions (approved / rejected / withdrawn)
/// stamp `reviewed_at` and the reviewer's `identity.users.id`. The
/// `submitted` and `in_review` states leave them null.
enum ApplicationStatus {
  submitted
  in_review
  approved
  rejected
  withdrawn

  @@map("application_status")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Checkr background-check status (TS-051). Mirrors the Checkr
/// catalog of report statuses with two locally-introduced shapes:
///
///   `pending`     = row inserted, no Checkr response yet (the
///                   row exists before the candidate-create call
///                   completes — covers the brief window between
///                   row insert and Checkr SDK call). The default
///                   state on insert.
///   `processing`  = Checkr accepted the report; results are still
///                   being computed.
///   `clear`       = Checkr cleared the candidate; no adverse
///                   findings.
///   `consider`    = Checkr returned adverse findings; ops manual
///                   review required.
///   `suspended`   = Checkr-side suspended (typically when the
///                   account requires additional documentation).
///   `engaged`     = Continuous-monitoring enrolment is active.
///   `dispute`     = Candidate is disputing findings via Checkr's
///                   dispute flow.
///   `canceled`    = Report canceled before completion (operator-
///                   initiated or candidate-initiated).
///   `failed`      = Checkr couldn't complete the report (e.g.
///                   candidate did not finish providing required
///                   info within the window).
///
/// Provider-tier promotion (TS-052) gates on the combined
/// `clear` ∧ KYC-verified condition.
enum BackgroundCheckStatus {
  pending
  processing
  clear
  consider
  suspended
  engaged
  dispute
  canceled
  failed

  @@map("background_check_status")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider — the row that backs every chef / culinary-companion /
/// caregiver listing on the platform.
///
/// One provider per identity user (the `user_id` column is unique). A
/// human can hold at most one provider account; staff who also
/// subscribe as a family payer wear two different rows in two
/// different services.
///
/// **Storage shape rationale** (CLAUDE.md §17.1; PDD §16.3 / §21.3).
/// The profile holds business-marketing-shaped content (name,
/// headline, bio, photo, video) plus operational pricing metadata
/// (tier, status). Strictly-sensitive material (full DOB, SSN-for-
/// Checkr, payout bank account) does NOT live on this row — that
/// data class belongs to service-identity (KYC light, TS-026), the
/// soon-to-arrive `provider_documents` table (background-check
/// artefacts, TS-052), and service-payouts (Stripe Connect account,
/// TS-090). Keeping the public profile row narrow bounds the blast
/// radius of an accidental read-path leak and keeps the search-
/// indexer's projection cheap.
///
/// `display_name` is the public-facing name on the profile card. It
/// can differ from the legal name on the KYC record (chefs frequently
/// brand under a "Chef <FirstName>" stage name). Family-portal
/// renders this verbatim.
///
/// `headline` is a short tagline (≤120 chars at the contract layer).
/// Drives the search result card and the social-share preview.
///
/// `bio` is a longer free-form body (≤4000 chars at the contract
/// layer). Markdown-friendly; rendered to HTML at the family-portal
/// boundary with strict sanitisation (CLAUDE.md §3.9 — no auto-render
/// of LLM / user-supplied content as raw HTML).
///
/// `profile_photo_key` / `video_intro_key` are forward references to
/// TS-110 `media-svc`. Stored as TEXT pointers with no FK or content
/// guarantee — when media-svc lands, the contract gate requires each
/// key to belong to a successfully-scanned asset before the family-
/// portal renders the asset (CLAUDE.md §3.4 mandatory pipeline).
/// Today the portal treats a non-null key as "asset uploaded; render
/// placeholder until media-svc validates".
///
/// `time_zone` is a required IANA zone (e.g. "America/New_York").
/// The availability service (TS-053) uses it to interpret weekday/
/// time-of-day windows; defaulting to the provider's signup-time
/// browser timezone is a TS-122 (web-provider) concern, not a schema
/// default.
///
/// Soft-delete preserves the audit history of every provider who has
/// ever served a booking — accounting reads (PDD §11.2 / Appendix A)
/// and compliance reads (PDD §17) must remain queryable after
/// archive.
model Provider {
  id                String         @id @default(cuid())
  /// Soft FK into `identity.users.id`. Never declare a Prisma
  /// relation here — cross-service relations would couple the two
  /// services' Prisma clients and violate CLAUDE.md §2.3 / §4.1.
  ///
  /// Unique: at most one provider profile per identity user. The
  /// `@unique` here mirrors the application-layer invariant; a
  /// DB-level guard against accidental double-create races is worth
  /// the index footprint cost given the row count is bounded by
  /// Year-1 ≈ 200 (PRD §12.2).
  userId            String         @unique @map("user_id")
  status            ProviderStatus @default(pending)
  tier              ProviderTier   @default(basic)
  displayName       String         @map("display_name")
  headline          String?
  bio               String?
  profilePhotoKey   String?        @map("profile_photo_key")
  videoIntroKey     String?        @map("video_intro_key")
  timeZone          String         @map("time_zone")
  /// TS-200 — Boolean flag set when the provider claims dementia-
  /// sensitive dining specialty. Default `false` so existing rows
  /// backfill with a safe "no claim either way" baseline; the
  /// provider re-confirms via the editor (`PUT /api/v1/providers/
  /// :id/profile`). The family-portal search filter on dementia-
  /// sensitive providers (PRD §6.1 / §6.3) reads this column via
  /// the discovery doc projection (TS-111).
  dementiaSensitive Boolean        @default(false) @map("dementia_sensitive")
  /// TS-204 — provider's self-set hourly rate, in the row's
  /// `hourly_rate_currency`. `Decimal(12,2)` per CLAUDE.md §4.1 —
  /// **never a float**. Nullable: a freshly-onboarded provider has no
  /// rate until they set one via `PUT /api/v1/providers/:id/pricing`.
  /// The write path enforces the rate sits inside the platform-set
  /// band for the provider's `tier` (PRD §5.2 / §7.2; the band lives
  /// in the contract layer's `PROVIDER_PRICING_BANDS` until TS-204-
  /// followup-2 moves it into a configurable `service_catalog` row).
  /// The rate flows into booking quotes (`booking.base_price`) once
  /// TS-204-followup-1 wires the cross-service read.
  hourlyRate        Decimal?       @map("hourly_rate") @db.Decimal(12, 2)
  /// TS-204 — ISO-4217 currency for `hourly_rate`. Set / cleared in
  /// lockstep with the rate (a half-populated pair is a data bug the
  /// service never writes). Phase-1 USD-only (PRD §11.4); the column
  /// is `Char(3)` so the multi-currency rollout (Phase 3) needs no
  /// migration.
  hourlyRateCurrency String?       @map("hourly_rate_currency") @db.Char(3)
  createdAt         DateTime       @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt         DateTime       @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  deletedAt         DateTime?      @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  @@index([status], map: "providers_status_idx")
  @@index([tier], map: "providers_tier_idx")
  @@index([deletedAt], map: "providers_deleted_at_idx")
  @@map("providers")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Polymorphic profile tag (TS-200). The single source of truth for
/// the provider's language / cuisine / dietary-expertise tag sets
/// edited by `PUT /api/v1/providers/:providerId/profile`.
///
/// **Why polymorphic over three sibling tables**. PDD §8.2 framed
/// these as `provider_languages` / `provider_cuisines` /
/// `provider_dietary_expertise`. Collapsing to one table:
///   - One migration to evolve (e.g. adding `created_by_user_id`
///     to all three; tracking soft-delete).
///   - One repository + one outbox event composition.
///   - The discovery doc (TS-111) projects flat tag arrays per
///     kind — the polymorphic shape aligns 1:1 with the doc's
///     projection.
///
/// **Tag string discipline**. The contract layer
/// (`provider-profile.schema.ts`) enforces lowercase + hyphen +
/// underscore, max 48 chars. The DB-level `kind` enum constrains
/// the discriminator so a controller bypass (admin tooling, repair
/// script) hits a 23-error on a malformed kind. Per-(provider,
/// kind, tag) uniqueness is enforced by the partial-free unique
/// index `provider_profile_tags_unique_idx` (see migration
/// `20260520140000_add_profile_tags_and_dementia_sensitive`).
///
/// **No FK to providers** — soft FK convention per CLAUDE.md §4.1.
/// Hard-deleting a provider should not orphan tag rows, but Phase-1
/// never hard-deletes providers (soft-delete via
/// `providers.deleted_at`). When a future cleanup tool needs to
/// purge tag rows for a hard-deleted provider, a one-off DELETE WHERE
/// provider_id NOT IN (SELECT id FROM providers) suffices.
model ProviderProfileTag {
  id         String                 @id @default(cuid())
  providerId String                 @map("provider_id")
  kind       ProviderProfileTagKind
  tag        String
  createdAt  DateTime               @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  @@unique([providerId, kind, tag], map: "provider_profile_tags_unique_idx")
  @@index([providerId, kind], map: "provider_profile_tags_provider_kind_idx")
  @@index([kind, tag], map: "provider_profile_tags_kind_tag_idx")
  @@map("provider_profile_tags")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// TS-200 — Tag kind discriminator for the polymorphic
/// `provider_profile_tags` table. Mirrors the contract-layer
/// `ProviderProfileTagKindSchema`.
///
///   `language`           — BCP-47 / ISO 639-1 language specifier.
///   `cuisine`            — free-text cuisine identifier.
///   `dietary_expertise`  — free-text dietary skill identifier.
enum ProviderProfileTagKind {
  language
  cuisine
  dietary_expertise

  @@map("provider_profile_tag_kind")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider application — one row per formal application submission.
///
/// **Lifecycle**. A row is inserted at application-submit time with
/// `status = submitted`. Ops review (TS-052) transitions to
/// `in_review` then to one of the terminal states (`approved` /
/// `rejected`). The applicant can withdraw before a terminal state
/// is reached, transitioning to `withdrawn`.
///
/// **Why a separate row from `providers`**. Each application is an
/// auditable event — the platform needs to keep a record of every
/// attempt, including rejections, for compliance and pattern-of-
/// abuse detection. A provider who applies, gets rejected, and
/// re-applies six months later creates two `provider_applications`
/// rows with the same `providers.id`. The applicant-side notes,
/// ops-side reviewer notes, and the per-attempt background-check row
/// (`provider_background_checks`) all hang off this row.
///
/// **No applicant PII** lives on this row. The applicant's legal
/// name, DOB, SSN-for-Checkr, and address travel directly to Checkr
/// at submission time; only the opaque `checkr_candidate_id` lands
/// on the matching `provider_background_checks` row. CLAUDE.md
/// §17.1.
model ProviderApplication {
  id             String            @id @default(cuid())
  /// Soft FK into the local `providers.id`. The Prisma relation is
  /// declared here (same-schema FK is fine — CLAUDE.md §4.1 bars
  /// only cross-service FKs).
  providerId     String            @map("provider_id")
  status         ApplicationStatus @default(submitted)
  /// Optional free-text from the applicant ("I worked at Daniel for
  /// six years and want to make this my full-time career"). Bounded
  /// at the contract layer; UI surfaces it on the ops review page.
  applicantNotes String?           @map("applicant_notes")
  /// Soft FK into `identity.users.id`. Set when an ops admin reviews
  /// the application. Null while `submitted` / `in_review`.
  reviewerUserId String?           @map("reviewer_user_id")
  /// Free-text ops notes captured at review time. Audit-logged at
  /// the admin-tooling layer (TS-052 / TS-100).
  reviewNotes    String?           @map("review_notes")
  submittedAt    DateTime          @default(now()) @map("submitted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  reviewedAt     DateTime?         @map("reviewed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  withdrawnAt    DateTime?         @map("withdrawn_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  createdAt      DateTime          @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt      DateTime          @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Powers "show me this provider's most recent application" — the
  /// dominant read path for the provider-portal `GET .../me`
  /// endpoint and for the ops review queue.
  @@index([providerId, submittedAt(sort: Desc)], map: "provider_applications_provider_recent_idx")
  /// Powers the ops queue ("show me every application in
  /// `submitted` waiting for review").
  @@index([status], map: "provider_applications_status_idx")
  @@map("provider_applications")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider background check — Checkr lifecycle row.
///
/// **One row per Checkr report**. Each `ProviderApplication` typically
/// has one matching `ProviderBackgroundCheck` row; continuous
/// monitoring (TS-051-followup) would land additional rows as Checkr
/// emits periodic re-checks.
///
/// **Webhook idempotency**. Stripe Identity's idempotency key is
/// `event.id`; Checkr's is also `event.id` but the relationship to
/// the report is via `report.id` not the candidate-level id.
/// `lastEventId` tracks the most-recent Checkr event applied to
/// this row; the matching `applyWebhookEvent` short-circuits when
/// the same `event.id` is redelivered.
///
/// **Encrypted payload columns** (`payload_*`). The raw Checkr event
/// (which can include adverse-findings detail — county records,
/// names, dates) is encrypted at-rest under
/// `BACKGROUND_CHECK_PAYLOAD_ENC_KEY`. The cipher key is
/// **independent** from any other cipher in the codebase (CLAUDE.md
/// §3.5 compartmentalisation — a leaked KYC cipher key does NOT
/// grant the ability to read background-check payloads, and vice
/// versa). Columns are nullable so a freshly-inserted `pending` row
/// has no payload yet; on the first webhook event all four columns
/// transition to non-null together.
///
/// **No applicant PII** lives in clear columns. The Checkr candidate
/// id is opaque; the report id is opaque. The names / DOBs / SSNs
/// the applicant submitted live only inside the encrypted blob and
/// inside Checkr's own systems.
model ProviderBackgroundCheck {
  id                String                @id @default(cuid())
  /// Soft FK into the local `providers.id`. Same-schema FK is fine
  /// (CLAUDE.md §4.1 bars only cross-service FKs).
  providerId        String                @map("provider_id")
  /// Soft FK into the local `provider_applications.id`. Nullable
  /// because future continuous-monitoring re-checks (TS-051-followup)
  /// may not pair with a fresh application row.
  applicationId     String?               @map("application_id")
  status            BackgroundCheckStatus @default(pending)
  /// Checkr's `candidate.id` — opaque; the natural lookup key for
  /// the create-report → events-on-report flow.
  checkrCandidateId String                @unique @map("checkr_candidate_id")
  /// Checkr's `report.id` — opaque; set after the create-report
  /// call returns. The natural lookup key for the inbound webhook
  /// events; unique when set (an event references exactly one
  /// report row).
  checkrReportId    String?               @unique @map("checkr_report_id")
  /// Last applied Checkr `event.id`. Idempotency on webhook replay.
  lastEventId       String?               @map("last_event_id")
  /// Set exactly once on the transition into a terminal status
  /// (`clear` | `consider` | `failed` | `canceled`). Preserved
  /// across redelivered events so admin tooling has a stable
  /// "completed-as-of-when" answer.
  completedAt       DateTime?             @map("completed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// AES-256-GCM ciphertext of the JSON-stringified Checkr event
  /// payload. Nullable — populated on the first webhook event.
  payloadCiphertext Bytes?                @map("payload_ciphertext")
  /// 96-bit random IV used for the GCM encryption.
  payloadIv         Bytes?                @map("payload_iv")
  /// 128-bit GCM authentication tag.
  payloadAuthTag    Bytes?                @map("payload_auth_tag")
  /// Key version used for the encryption — supports forward-
  /// compatible key rotation via TS-051-followup-N's backfill
  /// worker (mirrors TS-026-followup-2 / TS-031-followup-3).
  payloadKeyVersion Int?                  @map("payload_key_version")
  createdAt         DateTime              @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt         DateTime              @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Powers "show me this provider's most recent background check" —
  /// the provider-portal `GET .../me` endpoint and the ops review
  /// page.
  @@index([providerId, createdAt(sort: Desc)], map: "provider_background_checks_provider_recent_idx")
  /// Powers the ops queue ("show me every check currently
  /// `consider`-state awaiting manual review").
  @@index([status], map: "provider_background_checks_status_idx")
  @@map("provider_background_checks")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Tier-transition reason (TS-052). Captures *why* a tier moved.
///
///   `auto_evaluation` — `TierPromotionService.evaluateAndApply`
///                       recomputed eligibility from the provider's
///                       active certifications + status and applied
///                       the resulting tier. The driver was almost
///                       always a cert grant / revoke / expiry; the
///                       exact upstream is captured by the actor in
///                       `triggered_by_user_id` plus the audit log
///                       at the calling endpoint.
///   `admin_override`  — `TierPromotionService.overrideTier` set
///                       the tier directly. Used when ops needs to
///                       suspend a tier (e.g. complaint review) or
///                       restore a tier outside the certification
///                       rules. Requires the `provider:approve`
///                       permission at the controller boundary.
///
/// Kept narrow on purpose. More-granular distinctions
/// (`certification_granted` vs `certification_revoked`) are derivable
/// from the actor + endpoint logs, and a richer enum would force a
/// schema change every time a new trigger lands.
enum TierTransitionReason {
  auto_evaluation
  admin_override

  @@map("tier_transition_reason")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Certification catalog (TS-052). One row per credential the
/// platform recognises.
///
/// **What lives here**. The cooking-academy certifications Taste &
/// See issues plus any third-party credentials we honour. Phase-1
/// seed (PRD §5.2 + §9):
///   - `ccc` — Certified Culinary Companion (gates `certified` tier)
///   - `ecc` — Elite Concierge Provider (gates `elite` tier alongside CCC)
///   - `dementia_sensitive` — Dementia-Sensitive Dining (specialty)
///   - `therapeutic_meals` — Therapeutic Meals (specialty)
///
/// **`code` discipline**. Stable forever — referenced by tier rules
/// and by every issuance row. To retire a certification, flip
/// `active = false` and stop offering it; never reuse a code.
///
/// **`default_validity_months`**. When set, `provider_certifications`
/// rows for this cert auto-compute an `expires_at` from `issued_at +
/// validity_months`. NULL means no expiry — appropriate for one-shot
/// credentials with no renewal cycle. Gate certs (CCC, ECC) ship
/// with a 24-month renewal cycle (PRD §9.3 / §15.2); specialty certs
/// can ship with longer cycles or no expiry depending on the
/// program design.
///
/// **Lifecycle**. The seed function (`seedCertificationsCatalog`)
/// upserts on `code` so a rerun against a hand-edited row only
/// refreshes the columns the catalog owns. Operators retire a cert
/// by editing `active = false`; existing issuance rows continue to
/// reference the catalog row.
model Certification {
  id                    String   @id @default(cuid())
  code                  String   @unique
  name                  String
  description           String
  issuer                String
  defaultValidityMonths Int?     @map("default_validity_months")
  sortPosition          Int      @default(0) @map("sort_position")
  active                Boolean  @default(true)
  createdAt             DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt             DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  @@map("certifications")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider certification issuance (TS-052) — one row per grant of a
/// credential to a provider.
///
/// **Lifecycle**. `issued_at` stamps creation; `expires_at` is
/// optional (set when the catalog's `default_validity_months` is
/// non-null, or supplied directly by the granter); `revoked_at` is
/// set on explicit revoke. A cert is **active** when
/// `revoked_at IS NULL AND (expires_at IS NULL OR expires_at > now)`.
///
/// **Idempotency**. The partial unique index
/// `provider_certifications_active_unique_idx` enforces "at most one
/// active row per (provider, certification)" at the database level.
/// Re-granting after revoke creates a fresh row — the partial
/// predicate scopes uniqueness to the active set so the audit trail
/// is preserved.
///
/// **Soft-FK convention**. `certificationId` is a soft FK into the
/// same-schema `certifications.id` (same-schema FK is permitted per
/// CLAUDE.md §4.1). `issuerUserId` and `revokerUserId` are soft FKs
/// into `identity.users.id` and are not declared as Prisma
/// relations.
model ProviderCertification {
  id               String    @id @default(cuid())
  providerId       String    @map("provider_id")
  certificationId  String    @map("certification_id")
  issuedAt         DateTime  @default(now()) @map("issued_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  expiresAt        DateTime? @map("expires_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  revokedAt        DateTime? @map("revoked_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  revocationReason String?   @map("revocation_reason")
  issuerUserId     String?   @map("issuer_user_id")
  revokerUserId    String?   @map("revoker_user_id")
  notes            String?
  createdAt        DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt        DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Dominant read path — `listForProvider(providerId)`.
  @@index([providerId], map: "provider_certifications_provider_idx")
  /// Powers "every provider who holds <cert>" — used by the search
  /// indexer (TS-053) and ops reporting.
  @@index([certificationId], map: "provider_certifications_certification_idx")
  @@map("provider_certifications")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Outbox event (TS-142-followup-1, PDD §7.3, CLAUDE.md §5.3).
///
/// Producer-side row written in the same Prisma transaction as the
/// business state change. The `apps/workers/outbox-relay` worker
/// polls for rows with `dispatched_at IS NULL`, forwards them to
/// Redis Streams (`events:{event_name}`), and stamps `dispatched_at`
/// on success. Consumers must be idempotent on `event_id`
/// (at-least-once delivery semantics).
///
/// Column shape is identical across every producer service — each
/// service ships its own migration that creates a separate
/// `{schema}.outbox_events` table. The `@taste-and-see/nest-outbox`
/// SDK is schema-agnostic; services compose `OutboxModule.forRoot`
/// with their schema name and the SDK writes the row via raw SQL
/// inside the caller's `prisma.$transaction`.
///
/// The model is declared here primarily so `prisma generate` reflects
/// the table on the local client (for ad-hoc queries via Prisma
/// Studio + admin tooling). Service code writes via the SDK's raw
/// SQL surface; reads happen exclusively through the relay's
/// `pg`-backed claim repository (the worker app does not share this
/// Prisma client).
///
/// Provider-side producers (TS-052-followup-1):
///   - `provider.certification_granted`  — emitted by
///     ProviderCertificationsService.grant on a successful insert.
///   - `provider.certification_revoked`  — emitted by
///     ProviderCertificationsService.revoke on a successful revoke.
///   - `provider.tier_changed`           — emitted by
///     TierPromotionService.evaluateAndApply / overrideTier when a
///     tier transition actually lands a row in
///     `provider.provider_tier_history`. Consumed by
///     `apps/workers/search-indexer` (TS-053) to keep the
///     Elasticsearch provider-discovery doc fresh.
model OutboxEvent {
  /// Dedup key — consumers must idempotency-check on this. UUID v4
  /// generated by the SDK by default; producers may pass an explicit
  /// id (e.g. a deterministic `{providerId}.tier.{occurredAtEpochMs}`
  /// composite) for cross-boundary exactly-once-effective delivery.
  eventId         String    @id @map("event_id")
  /// Dot-notation event name from `packages/contracts/events`. The
  /// SDK validates the name against the registry before inserting
  /// — unknown names are rejected at the producer boundary.
  eventName       String    @map("event_name")
  /// Validated domain payload (validated by the SDK against the
  /// matching `eventRegistry` schema before insert).
  payload         Json
  /// Producer wall-clock time the event semantically happened. May
  /// differ from `created_at` for backfilled events.
  occurredAt      DateTime  @map("occurred_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// Service name that produced the row (e.g. `service-provider`).
  /// Useful for relay observability + per-service throughput metrics.
  producerService String    @map("producer_service")
  createdAt       DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// Stamped by the relay when the row has been successfully
  /// forwarded onto the bus. Null means undispatched + eligible for
  /// the next poll cycle.
  dispatchedAt    DateTime? @map("dispatched_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// Failed dispatch counter. The relay stops retrying once this
  /// reaches `MAX_ATTEMPTS` — those rows are dead-lettered for ops
  /// attention (surfaced via metrics + an admin endpoint).
  attempts        Int       @default(0)
  /// Stamped by the relay on the most recent failed attempt.
  lastAttemptAt   DateTime? @map("last_attempt_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// Most recent failure message — capped at 2000 chars at the
  /// relay's record-failure path.
  lastError       String?   @map("last_error")

  /// Relay's dominant read query: "give me the next batch of
  /// undispatched rows ordered by created_at". A partial index on
  /// `dispatched_at IS NULL` keeps the index footprint bounded
  /// against an ever-growing table (the table is append-only; the
  /// partial filter is highly selective at steady state).
  ///
  /// Prisma's `@@index` cannot directly express the partial
  /// predicate; the partial index is materialised in the migration
  /// SQL (20260516120000_outbox_events).
  @@index([eventName], map: "outbox_events_event_name_idx")
  @@map("outbox_events")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider tier history (TS-052) — append-only audit of every tier
/// transition.
///
/// **Append-only invariant**. Rows are inserted and never updated or
/// deleted (CLAUDE.md §3.6 / §17.7). The TierPromotionService inserts
/// exactly one row per transition; admin tooling reads but never
/// mutates.
///
/// **`fromTier` nullable**. The first transition for a freshly-
/// created provider records the initial tier with `from_tier = NULL`.
/// In practice this rarely fires (providers default to `basic` at row
/// creation and most evaluations are no-ops until a certification
/// lands), but the column is kept nullable for cases where the
/// initial state was set out-of-band.
///
/// **No FK to the providers table** because the same-schema FK
/// constraint can break tier-history reads when a provider is hard-
/// deleted. The soft-FK pattern keeps history readable forever; ops
/// reads filter by `provider_id` and accept that an orphaned row is
/// orphaned data (in practice we never hard-delete providers — see
/// the `Provider.deletedAt` column).
model ProviderTierHistory {
  id                String               @id @default(cuid())
  providerId        String               @map("provider_id")
  fromTier          ProviderTier?        @map("from_tier")
  toTier            ProviderTier         @map("to_tier")
  reason            TierTransitionReason
  triggeredByUserId String?              @map("triggered_by_user_id")
  notes             String?
  occurredAt        DateTime             @default(now()) @map("occurred_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  createdAt         DateTime             @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Dominant read path — `getHistory(providerId)` returns the
  /// provider's transitions newest-first for the ops review page.
  @@index([providerId, occurredAt(sort: Desc)], map: "provider_tier_history_provider_occurred_idx")
  @@map("provider_tier_history")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Weekday literal (TS-203). Mirrors the contract-layer
/// `ProviderAvailabilityWeekdaySchema`. Lowercased English names
/// because the family-portal's locale-aware rendering happens at
/// the UI layer — the booking-svc availability gate matches on the
/// literal, so swapping to numeric 0..6 here would force the
/// downstream to translate.
enum ProviderAvailabilityWeekday {
  sunday
  monday
  tuesday
  wednesday
  thursday
  friday
  saturday

  @@map("provider_availability_weekday")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Recurring weekly availability window (TS-203). One row per
/// `(provider_id, weekday, start_time, end_time)` quadruple.
/// Multiple non-overlapping windows per weekday are allowed (e.g.
/// lunch + dinner shifts on the same day).
///
/// **Half-open interval semantics**. `[start_time, end_time)` — a
/// 09:00–13:00 window is inclusive of 09:00 and exclusive of 13:00.
/// Midnight-spanning windows are NOT supported by the contract
/// layer; the DB schema does not enforce the constraint (the
/// downstream contract refine catches it before any INSERT lands)
/// to keep the column shape simple.
///
/// **Time stored without timezone**. The provider's `time_zone`
/// column on `providers` carries the interpretation — when the
/// booking-svc availability gate (TS-060 / TS-205) reads a window,
/// it composes the parent provider's `time_zone` with the local
/// `start_time` / `end_time` to materialise a real instant. The
/// `@db.Time(0)` column is timezone-naive minute-precision.
///
/// **Uniqueness**. The partial-free unique index
/// `provider_availability_windows_unique_idx` enforces "at most one
/// row per (provider, weekday, start, end)" so a malformed direct
/// write surfaces as a 23505 PG unique-violation rather than
/// silently doubling a shift. Cross-window overlap detection
/// (Sunday 09:00–13:00 vs Sunday 12:00–14:00) lives at the contract
/// layer — the DB cannot express the overlap predicate without a
/// trigger.
///
/// **Soft-FK to providers** — same-schema FK is allowed per
/// CLAUDE.md §4.1, but the soft-FK pattern is consistent with the
/// rest of the provider domain and keeps history readable if a
/// provider is hard-deleted (which Phase-1 never does — `Provider.
/// deletedAt` is the canonical removal).
model ProviderAvailabilityWindow {
  id         String                      @id @default(cuid())
  providerId String                      @map("provider_id")
  weekday    ProviderAvailabilityWeekday
  /// Local-time inclusive start (minute precision; no seconds).
  startTime  DateTime                    @map("start_time") @db.Time(0)
  /// Local-time exclusive end (minute precision; no seconds). The
  /// contract layer enforces `startTime < endTime`.
  endTime    DateTime                    @map("end_time") @db.Time(0)
  createdAt  DateTime                    @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt  DateTime                    @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Dominant read path — `getAvailability(providerId)` returns the
  /// full set of windows for one provider, partitioned by weekday
  /// client-side. The `(provider_id, weekday, start_time)` shape
  /// also serves the booking-svc availability gate's
  /// "does this provider have a slot covering 2026-05-21 11:30?"
  /// query with index-only scans.
  @@index([providerId, weekday, startTime], map: "provider_availability_windows_provider_weekday_idx")
  /// Defence-in-depth dedup at the DB layer — the contract layer
  /// rejects overlapping windows + duplicates at the boundary.
  @@unique([providerId, weekday, startTime, endTime], map: "provider_availability_windows_unique_idx")
  @@map("provider_availability_windows")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Date-keyed availability exception (TS-203). One row per
/// `(provider_id, exception_date)` pair. The provider declares "I
/// am not available on this date" — every recurring window
/// scheduled for that date is treated as blocked. Partial-day
/// blocks + per-date overrides are NOT modelled in this phase
/// (TS-203-followup-1 lands them).
///
/// **Date stored as PG `date`**. Calendar date in the provider's
/// local timezone; no time-of-day component. The booking-svc
/// availability gate reads the date verbatim against the same
/// timezone-naive comparison the recurring windows use.
///
/// **Uniqueness**. One exception per (provider, date) pair.
/// Duplicate writes surface as a 23505 PG unique-violation.
model ProviderAvailabilityException {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  providerId    String   @map("provider_id")
  /// Date in the provider's local timezone (calendar date only).
  exceptionDate DateTime @map("exception_date") @db.Date
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Dominant read path — `getAvailability(providerId)` returns the
  /// full set of exceptions for one provider, sorted by date. The
  /// booking-svc availability gate also reads with this index when
  /// resolving "is YYYY-MM-DD blocked for this provider?".
  @@index([providerId, exceptionDate], map: "provider_availability_exceptions_provider_date_idx")
  /// Defence-in-depth dedup at the DB layer — the contract layer
  /// rejects duplicate dates at the boundary.
  @@unique([providerId, exceptionDate], map: "provider_availability_exceptions_unique_idx")
  @@map("provider_availability_exceptions")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider service area (TS-202). One row per declared coverage
/// polygon. A provider serving both Manhattan's Upper East Side and
/// parts of Brooklyn declares two rows.
///
/// **GeoJSON, not PostGIS** (Phase-1 scope choice). `geo_polygon`
/// stores the GeoJSON `Polygon` (RFC 7946) verbatim as `jsonb`. The
/// `centroid_*` + `bbox_*` columns are the planar area-weighted
/// centroid + axis-aligned bounding box, computed in
/// `ServiceAreasService` at write time and persisted so the
/// search-indexer (TS-053-followup-3) + the household-in-service-area
/// gate (TS-210) can read them without re-parsing the polygon. No
/// PostGIS extension is required on the Phase-1 self-managed Postgres;
/// polygon-intersection search lands with the live Elasticsearch geo
/// wiring (TS-210).
///
/// **Float columns are geographic coordinates, NOT money** — the
/// CLAUDE.md §4.1 "no floats for money" rule does not apply. WGS84
/// decimal degrees in `double precision` is the conventional geo
/// representation; six fractional digits (~10 cm) is far inside
/// `double`'s precision.
///
/// **Soft-FK to providers** — same-schema FK is allowed per CLAUDE.md
/// §4.1, but the soft-FK pattern is consistent with the rest of the
/// provider domain and keeps history readable if a provider is
/// hard-deleted (which Phase-1 never does — `Provider.deletedAt` is
/// the canonical removal).
///
/// **Full-set replace semantics** — the editor PUTs the complete set;
/// the service DELETEs every row for the provider then bulk-inserts
/// the new set inside one transaction. No per-row uniqueness
/// constraint: two identical polygons are a client error the contract
/// layer does not (yet) reject, but a duplicate coverage area is
/// harmless to the centroid + radius scoring.
model ProviderServiceArea {
  id         String @id @default(cuid())
  providerId String @map("provider_id")
  /// Optional human label ("Upper East Side"). Null = unlabelled.
  label      String?
  /// GeoJSON `Polygon` (RFC 7946) stored verbatim. `coordinates[0]`
  /// is the exterior ring; subsequent rings are holes. Positions are
  /// `[longitude, latitude]`.
  geoPolygon Json   @map("geo_polygon")
  /// Planar area-weighted centroid of the exterior ring (WGS84 decimal
  /// degrees). Read by the discovery-doc projection (TS-053-followup-3).
  centroidLatitude  Float @map("centroid_latitude")
  centroidLongitude Float @map("centroid_longitude")
  /// Axis-aligned bounding box (WGS84 decimal degrees). `min* <= max*`
  /// guaranteed by the producer. A cheap pre-filter for the
  /// household-in-service-area gate (TS-210).
  bboxMinLatitude   Float @map("bbox_min_latitude")
  bboxMinLongitude  Float @map("bbox_min_longitude")
  bboxMaxLatitude   Float @map("bbox_max_latitude")
  bboxMaxLongitude  Float @map("bbox_max_longitude")
  createdAt  DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt  DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Dominant read path — `getServiceAreas(providerId)` returns the
  /// full set of areas for one provider. The discovery-snapshot
  /// projection (TS-053-followup-3) reads the same way.
  @@index([providerId], map: "provider_service_areas_provider_idx")
  @@map("provider_service_areas")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// External calendar provider (TS-206). Mirrors the contract-layer
/// `ProviderCalendarProviderSchema`. Phase-1 ships `google` only;
/// `icloud` (CalDAV) + `outlook` (Microsoft Graph) append — never
/// reorder — with TS-206-followup-2.
enum ProviderCalendarProvider {
  google

  @@map("provider_calendar_provider")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// External-calendar connection health (TS-206). Mirrors the contract-
/// layer `ProviderCalendarConnectionStatusSchema`.
///
///   `connected` — refresh token held + last free/busy pull succeeded.
///   `error`     — last sync failed (Google rejected the refresh token:
///                 the provider revoked access on Google's side, or the
///                 grant expired). The provider must reconnect; the
///                 reconsent prompt is TS-206-followup-5.
///
/// A disconnected provider has NO row at all (the DELETE drops it), so
/// there is no `disconnected` member.
enum ProviderCalendarConnectionStatus {
  connected
  error

  @@map("provider_calendar_connection_status")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider external-calendar connection (TS-206). One row per
/// (provider, external calendar) — Phase-1 one Google connection per
/// provider, enforced by the `provider_id` unique index (the
/// `calendar_provider` column is forward-compat for the iCloud /
/// Outlook siblings; when a provider can link more than one external
/// calendar, the unique moves to `(provider_id, calendar_provider)`).
///
/// **Refresh token at rest is encrypted** (AES-256-GCM) under
/// `CALENDAR_TOKEN_ENC_KEY` — an **independent** key from every other
/// cipher in the codebase (CLAUDE.md §3.5 compartmentalisation). The
/// four `refresh_token_*` columns mirror the `provider_background_checks.
/// payload_*` shape (ciphertext + 96-bit IV + 128-bit GCM tag + key
/// version). The refresh token is a long-lived secret — never logged
/// (CLAUDE.md §3.9 / §17.2). Columns are non-null: a connection row is
/// only ever written with a freshly-encrypted token.
///
/// **`connected_account_email`** is low-sensitivity (the provider's own
/// Google account address, captured from the `openid email` claim) and
/// stored in clear for display in the portal. Nullable — a re-grant may
/// omit the claim.
///
/// **No event content** is stored here — busy intervals live in the
/// separate `provider_availability_external` mirror; this row holds only
/// the connection state + the encrypted token.
///
/// **Soft-FK to providers** — same-schema FK is allowed (CLAUDE.md §4.1)
/// but the soft-FK pattern is consistent with the rest of the provider
/// domain.
model ProviderCalendarConnection {
  id                    String                           @id @default(cuid())
  providerId            String                           @unique @map("provider_id")
  calendarProvider      ProviderCalendarProvider         @default(google) @map("calendar_provider")
  status                ProviderCalendarConnectionStatus @default(connected)
  /// Low-sensitivity Google account address (display only). Nullable.
  connectedAccountEmail String?                          @map("connected_account_email")
  /// OAuth scope string granted at consent time. Stored for audit /
  /// debugging ("does this grant still carry the freebusy scope?").
  grantedScope          String?                          @map("granted_scope")
  /// AES-256-GCM ciphertext of the Google refresh token.
  refreshTokenCiphertext Bytes                           @map("refresh_token_ciphertext")
  /// 96-bit random IV used for the GCM encryption.
  refreshTokenIv        Bytes                            @map("refresh_token_iv")
  /// 128-bit GCM authentication tag.
  refreshTokenAuthTag   Bytes                            @map("refresh_token_auth_tag")
  /// Key version used for the encryption — forward-compatible rotation
  /// via TS-206-followup-5's backfill worker.
  refreshTokenKeyVersion Int                             @map("refresh_token_key_version")
  /// Last successful free/busy pull. Null until the first sync lands.
  lastSyncedAt          DateTime?                        @map("last_synced_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// Last failure message when `status = error`; null when healthy.
  /// Capped at 512 chars at the service boundary.
  lastSyncError         String?                          @map("last_sync_error")
  createdAt             DateTime                         @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt             DateTime                         @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  @@map("provider_calendar_connections")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Provider external availability busy mirror (TS-206) — the read-only
/// cache of busy intervals pulled from the provider's external calendar
/// via the free/busy API. The availability projection (TS-203
/// `resolveNextSevenDays`) unions these intervals with the recurring
/// windows + date exclusions: a window occurrence that overlaps an
/// external busy block is treated as unavailable.
///
/// **Derived + re-buildable.** A sync DELETEs every row for the provider
/// then bulk-inserts the fresh set inside one transaction; dropping +
/// re-syncing is always safe (the source of truth is the external
/// calendar). The table name `provider_availability_external` matches
/// the TS-206 acceptance wording.
///
/// **No event content** — only opaque absolute `[starts_at, ends_at)`
/// UTC instants + the source. Never a title / attendee / location /
/// description (the OAuth scope is free/busy-only; ADR-0003).
///
/// **Absolute UTC instants.** Unlike the recurring `provider_availability
/// _windows` (local wall-clock `time(0)`), external busy intervals are
/// concrete instants from the external calendar, stored as
/// `timestamptz`. The availability-union helper converts the local
/// recurring windows into UTC instants (using the provider's `time_zone`)
/// before testing overlap.
///
/// **Soft-FK to providers** — soft-FK convention per CLAUDE.md §4.1.
model ProviderCalendarExternalBusy {
  id               String                   @id @default(cuid())
  providerId       String                   @map("provider_id")
  source           ProviderCalendarProvider @default(google)
  startsAt         DateTime                 @map("starts_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  endsAt           DateTime                 @map("ends_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// When this busy interval was last (re-)synced into the mirror. All
  /// rows from one sync carry the same timestamp.
  syncedAt         DateTime                 @default(now()) @map("synced_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  createdAt        DateTime                 @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// Dominant read path — `getExternalBusyIntervals(providerId)` returns
  /// the provider's mirrored intervals ordered by start. The
  /// `(provider_id, starts_at)` shape also serves a future booking-svc
  /// availability gate's "is this provider busy at instant T?" range
  /// scan (TS-206-followup-4).
  @@index([providerId, startsAt], map: "provider_availability_external_provider_starts_idx")
  @@map("provider_availability_external")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Consumer-side dedup ledger for `@taste-and-see/nest-outbox-consumer`
/// (TS-305d — service-provider's FIRST outbox-consumer surface; every
/// other table in this schema is producer-side or write-side master).
///
/// Mirrors the canonical copy in `service-accounting` /
/// `service-trust-safety`. The `state` CHECK constraint and the partial
/// index on `dead_lettered_at IS NOT NULL` live in the migration SQL,
/// not here — Prisma cannot express either.
///
/// **This table is a CACHE of processing decisions, not the idempotency
/// guarantee.** Truncate it, or rename the consumer group, and every
/// event replays. The durable guard for the metrics projection is that
/// each handler's write is *conditional* — it fills a column that is
/// still null and never overwrites a decided one — so replaying an
/// event is a no-op rather than a double count. See
/// `ProviderBookingFact`.
model OutboxConsumerDedup {
  consumerGroup  String    @map("consumer_group")
  eventId        String    @map("event_id")
  eventName      String    @map("event_name")
  state          String
  attempts       Int       @default(1)
  lastError      String?   @map("last_error")
  firstSeenAt    DateTime  @default(now()) @map("first_seen_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  lastAttemptAt  DateTime  @default(now()) @map("last_attempt_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  processedAt    DateTime? @map("processed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  deadLetteredAt DateTime? @map("dead_lettered_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  @@id([consumerGroup, eventId])
  @@map("outbox_consumer_dedup")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Per-booking projection of service-booking's lifecycle events — the
/// fact table `provider_metrics` is derived from (TS-305d).
///
/// **Why a per-booking row and not counters alone.** Two of the three
/// figures PDD §8.2 promises cannot be computed from counters:
///
///   - **Response time** needs the offer instant *and* the response
///     instant on the same booking. `booking.confirmed` carries
///     `confirmedAt` but NOT the original offer time, so the projector
///     has to retain `booking.created.occurredAt` somewhere. A row is
///     that somewhere.
///   - **A rolling window** cannot be decremented out of a counter
///     without the underlying dates. Keeping the dates makes any
///     window a query rather than a schema decision — which matters,
///     because 90 days is an unconfirmed product number (see
///     `PROVIDER_METRICS_WINDOW_DAYS` at the contract layer).
///
/// **This is a read model built from events, not a copy of another
/// service's table** (CLAUDE.md §2.3 forbids the latter). It carries no
/// household id, no senior id, no money and no free text — only what a
/// reliability aggregate needs. The booking's identity is kept solely
/// so the projection can be idempotent and order-independent.
///
/// **Every column is written CONDITIONALLY.** Each handler fills the
/// fields for its own lifecycle position, and only while they are still
/// unset, so a replayed event is a no-op and out-of-order events still
/// converge (a `booking.completed` seen before its `booking.created`
/// creates the row; the later `created` fills the offer instant). This
/// is the durable idempotency guard — `outbox_consumer_dedup` is only a
/// cache in front of it.
///
/// **What this table deliberately CANNOT answer: who caused a
/// cancellation.** `booking.canceled` carries `canceledByUserId`, but
/// service-provider cannot resolve whether that id is the provider, the
/// family payer or an admin without a cross-service read §2.3 forbids —
/// the same wall TS-308c hit. So the figure derived here is "bookings
/// cancelled after this provider accepted them", which is a fact, and
/// NOT "cancellations the provider caused", which would be a guess. The
/// contract's field names say so out loud.
model ProviderBookingFact {
  /// Soft FK into `booking.bookings.id`. The PK — one row per booking,
  /// which is what makes every handler an upsert.
  bookingId              String    @id @map("booking_id")
  /// Soft FK into `providers.id`. Present on every booking lifecycle
  /// event (`BookingIdentifiersSchema`), so any handler can create the
  /// row even when it is the first one seen for that booking.
  providerId             String    @map("provider_id")
  /// `BookingServiceKind` from the contract layer. TEXT rather than an
  /// enum: this is a projection of another context's vocabulary, and a
  /// new service kind there must not require a `CREATE TYPE` here.
  serviceKind            String?   @map("service_kind")
  /// `booking.created.occurredAt` — when the request reached the
  /// provider. Null when the `created` event has not been seen (an
  /// out-of-order delivery, or a booking that predates this
  /// projection); a null offer instant excludes the row from
  /// response-time statistics but NOT from completion statistics.
  offeredAt              DateTime? @map("offered_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// When the offer was answered — `confirmedAt` or `declinedAt`.
  respondedAt            DateTime? @map("responded_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// `accepted` | `declined`. Set together with `responded_at`, always.
  responseKind           String?   @map("response_kind")
  /// `provider_declined` | `window_expired` | `admin_declined` from
  /// `booking.declined`. Load-bearing, and the reason acceptance is not
  /// one number: `window_expired` is a provider who never answered,
  /// `admin_declined` is not the provider's act at all, and collapsing
  /// the three would score ops decisions against the provider.
  declineKind            String?   @map("decline_kind")
  /// Terminal lifecycle position: `completed` | `canceled` | `declined`.
  outcome                String?
  /// When the terminal position was reached.
  outcomeAt              DateTime? @map("outcome_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// `BookingCancellationReason` when `outcome = 'canceled'`. Kept
  /// because `no_show` is a materially different event from
  /// `family_schedule_change`, and one cancellation rate hides that.
  cancellationReason     String?   @map("cancellation_reason")
  /// `pending` | `confirmed` | `in_progress` — the status the booking
  /// was cancelled OUT OF. This is what makes a cancellation relatable
  /// to the acceptance at all: a booking cancelled from `pending` was
  /// never accepted, so it belongs to the offer funnel and not to
  /// reliability.
  canceledPreviousStatus String?   @map("canceled_previous_status")
  createdAt              DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt              DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  /// The dominant read path is always "this provider's facts, bounded
  /// by a date". `outcome_at` bounds the reliability figures;
  /// `offered_at` bounds the funnel figures, which the second index
  /// serves.
  @@index([providerId, outcomeAt], map: "provider_booking_facts_provider_outcome_idx")
  @@index([providerId, offeredAt], map: "provider_booking_facts_provider_offered_idx")
  @@map("provider_booking_facts")
  @@schema("provider")
}

/// Lifetime reliability rollup per provider (TS-305d) — the
/// `provider_metrics` table PDD §8.2 has named since TS-050.
///
/// **Only LIFETIME figures live here, and that is the whole design
/// decision.** A stored rolling-window figure is wrong the moment the
/// clock moves past its edge: a provider who stops working keeps a
/// flattering 90-day completion rate forever, because nothing arrives
/// to trigger a recompute. Lifetime counters have no such edge — they
/// change only when an event arrives, which is exactly when this row is
/// recomputed. The windowed figures are therefore derived at READ time
/// from `provider_booking_facts`, where they cannot go stale.
///
/// So the split is: this table exists for reads that span MANY
/// providers (search ranking, TS-053-followup-4's discovery document, a
/// future reliability-aware tier rule) where a per-provider aggregate
/// would be an N+1. A single-provider read (the dossier, the 360) goes
/// to the facts.
///
/// **Recomputed, never incremented.** Each handler re-derives this row
/// from that provider's facts inside the same transaction as the fact
/// write. An increment would drift the moment one event were replayed
/// or applied out of order; a recompute cannot.
///
/// **No rating columns.** PDD §8.2's sketch lists `rating_avg`, but
/// nothing on this platform captures a rating, and a nullable column
/// here would read as "this provider has no rating" when the truth is
/// "Taste and See does not collect ratings" (TS-305e). The absence is
/// deliberate, exactly as it is in the dossier contract.
model ProviderMetrics {
  /// Soft FK into `providers.id`. One row per provider, created on the
  /// first booking event seen for them.
  providerId                      String    @id @map("provider_id")
  /// Offers seen — rows with a known `offered_at`.
  bookingsOffered                 Int       @default(0) @map("bookings_offered")
  bookingsAccepted                Int       @default(0) @map("bookings_accepted")
  /// Declines the provider actually made (`decline_kind =
  /// 'provider_declined'`). Excludes `window_expired` and
  /// `admin_declined` — see `ProviderBookingFact.declineKind`.
  bookingsDeclined                Int       @default(0) @map("bookings_declined")
  /// Offers that lapsed unanswered (`decline_kind = 'window_expired'`).
  /// Counted separately because "said no" and "never answered" are
  /// different provider behaviours with different remedies.
  bookingsExpired                 Int       @default(0) @map("bookings_expired")
  bookingsCompleted               Int       @default(0) @map("bookings_completed")
  /// Bookings cancelled after this provider accepted them, by any
  /// party. NOT "cancellations the provider caused" — see the
  /// `ProviderBookingFact` doc-block.
  bookingsCanceledAfterAcceptance Int       @default(0) @map("bookings_canceled_after_acceptance")
  /// Sum and count of offer-to-response gaps, in whole seconds, over
  /// bookings where both instants are known. A MEAN is derivable from
  /// this pair; a MEDIAN is not — which is why the dossier's
  /// response-time figure is computed from the facts, and this pair
  /// serves only the many-provider reads.
  responseSecondsTotal            BigInt    @default(0) @map("response_seconds_total")
  responseSamples                 Int       @default(0) @map("response_samples")
  /// Earliest and latest fact observed. `firstObservedAt` tells a
  /// reader how far back the record actually goes, which a rate alone
  /// never does.
  firstObservedAt                 DateTime? @map("first_observed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  lastObservedAt                  DateTime? @map("last_observed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// When this rollup was last re-derived.
  computedAt                      DateTime  @default(now()) @map("computed_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  createdAt                       DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt                       DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  @@map("provider_metrics")
  @@schema("provider")
}
